{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2669 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "491e6cbb6b8bb6b54170b3bd230894b165804d7d0b2c2467a07d34d0d678307f" :edb/db-before-t 2668 :edb/db-after-t 2669 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535981 50 "2026-10-15T14:54:40.776Z" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068696 1038 17592186552462 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068696 1038 17592186552521 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068710 1038 17592186552472 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068711 1038 17592186552475 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068712 1038 17592186552406 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068749 1038 17592186552428 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068749 1038 17592186552435 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068749 1038 17592186552497 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186068910 1038 17592186552464 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186078508 1038 17592186552495 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186078508 1038 17592186552519 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186100952 1038 17592186552420 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186100952 1038 17592186552511 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186100953 1038 17592186552523 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1075 17592186113413 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186113412 1075 17592186113414 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186113412 1075 17592186113415 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186113412 1075 17592186113416 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186113412 1075 17592186113417 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186113412 1075 17592186551706 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1075 17592186551768 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1075 17592186551826 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1075 17592186551895 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1075 17592186551964 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186113412 1078 17592186551768 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 17592186552422 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 17592186552450 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115246 1038 17592186552453 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 17592186552440 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115247 1038 17592186552496 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 17592186552411 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 17592186552432 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 17592186552443 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115248 1038 17592186552525 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115326 1038 17592186552447 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115358 1038 17592186552414 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115358 1038 17592186552471 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115360 1038 17592186552415 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115741 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115741 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation $R_{1}:=R_{1} + 4R_{2}$ to a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$. Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186115741 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186115741 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186116497 1064 "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n![](images/25/25d4c31649834fe3ae42a701d49f6e4a31fa8626de1d9aeed0f98ccbbf4a57fb.svg)\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/49/497b76accb211bbe4253ee2094206f70e8856f9510db71f6367f255fcc565665.svg)\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186116497 1064 "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n![](images/25/25d4c31649834fe3ae42a701d49f6e4a31fa8626de1d9aeed0f98ccbbf4a57fb.svg)\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/49/497b76accb211bbe4253ee2094206f70e8856f9510db71f6367f255fcc565665.svg)\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186123026 1078 17592186123031 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186124528 1038 17592186552517 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186124579 1038 17592186552441 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186124605 1038 17592186552485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186124613 1038 17592186552439 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186125071 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186125071 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\cdot 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $λ(λ + 2)(λ - 1) = 0$\n2. $λ^{3} + 3λ^{2} - 2λ + 4 = 0$\n3. $λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} - 10λ = 0$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186125071 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $\\lambda (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nII. ${\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda =0$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186125071 1074 "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186128936 1071 "For the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The graph passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n2. The range of the function is $y \\in (-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n3. $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186128936 1071 "For the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The graph passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\nII. The range of the function is $y \\in (-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty).$\n\nIII. $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186128936 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bb7e6c981e7383bda679415437c5a250a82ff77e0708ac0f252f013ab38c25f0.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. When $x=4$, we get $y=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, so the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$.\n- Statement II is true. The curve attains every real number except $0$.\n- Statement III is false. From the graph, we see that $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186128936 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb7e6c981e7383bda679415437c5a250a82ff77e0708ac0f252f013ab38c25f0.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. When $x=4,$ we get $y=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ so the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ passes through the point $\\left(4,\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The curve attains every real number except $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. From the graph, we see that $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186128937 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$?\n\n![](images/2e/2e6d2bd2ccad040cbc29116cf30a9201cbe6649de7457f28221e2aca1393a857.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186128937 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}?$\n\n![](images/2e/2e6d2bd2ccad040cbc29116cf30a9201cbe6649de7457f28221e2aca1393a857.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186128937 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186128937 1074 "The graph of $y=\\dfrac1x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186129125 1038 17592186552444 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186129125 1038 17592186552493 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186129126 1038 17592186552509 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186129126 1038 17592186552520 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186129372 1050 420.0 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186130345 1064 "To plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwe first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nThen, we draw the graph by plotting the corresponding $(x,y)$ pairs and connecting them.\n\n![](images/3e/3eea82487a56147789d1c383c21e37c447dc94d735de094acd80183894462a38.png)\n\nThis type of curve is called a **rectangular hyperbola.**" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186130345 1064 "To plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nThen, we draw the graph by plotting the corresponding $(x,y)$ pairs and connecting them.\n\n![](images/3e/3eea82487a56147789d1c383c21e37c447dc94d735de094acd80183894462a38.png)\n\nThis type of curve is called a **rectangular hyperbola.**" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186130346 1064 "Let's now plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nOnce again, we first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/9a/9af11caef6739f83d32e0190ddc89c98b8e0150aa430184ee5e234a7febe8d8a.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis the reflection of the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nover the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/507a0a5cf7c2a1871efd76092c5164424ce17f3425c1bfc33df89cecefa81803.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186130346 1064 "Let's now plot the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Once again, we first create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/9a/9af11caef6739f83d32e0190ddc89c98b8e0150aa430184ee5e234a7febe8d8a.png)\n\nNotice that $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is the reflection of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ over the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/50/507a0a5cf7c2a1871efd76092c5164424ce17f3425c1bfc33df89cecefa81803.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186130430 1064 "Let's sketch the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nThere are some key points to note about this curve:\n\n- As $x \\to 0^+$, the graph approaches $\\infty$.\n- As $x \\to 0^-$, the graph approaches $-\\infty$.\n- The graph has the *vertical asymptote* $x=0$.\n- As $x\\to\\infty$ or $x\\to-\\infty$, the graph approaches the *horizontal asymptote* $y=0$.\n- The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$, and the range is $y\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n- The function is a decreasing function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186130430 1064 "Let's sketch the curve $y = \\dfrac1x.$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nThere are some key points to note about this curve:\n\n- As $x \\to 0^+,$ the graph approaches $\\infty.$\n\n- As $x \\to 0^-,$ the graph approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- The graph has the *vertical asymptote* $x=0.$\n\n- As $x\\to\\infty$ or $x\\to-\\infty,$ the graph approaches the *horizontal asymptote* $y=0.$\n\n- The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty),$ and the range is $y\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is a decreasing function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186130431 1064 "Now let's remind ourselves of the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5ed500c81d09e2775f09fd10a221d7ec0fca7e39249b7add7a680328b68c1.png)\n\nThere are some key points to note about this curve:\n\n- As $x\\to 0^+$, the graph approaches $-\\infty$.\n- As $x \\to 0^-$, the graph approaches $\\infty$.\n- The graph has the *vertical asymptote* $x=0$.\n- As $x\\to\\infty$ or $x\\to-\\infty$, the graph approaches the *horizontal asymptote* $y=0$.\n- The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$, and the range is $y\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n- The function is an increasing function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186130431 1064 "Now let's remind ourselves of the graph of $y = -\\dfrac1x.$\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5ed500c81d09e2775f09fd10a221d7ec0fca7e39249b7add7a680328b68c1.png)\n\nThere are some key points to note about this curve:\n\n- As $x\\to 0^+,$ the graph approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- As $x \\to 0^-,$ the graph approaches $\\infty.$\n\n- The graph has the *vertical asymptote* $x=0.$\n\n- As $x\\to\\infty$ or $x\\to-\\infty,$ the graph approaches the *horizontal asymptote* $y=0.$\n\n- The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty),$ and the range is $y\\in(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is an increasing function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1075 17592186132362 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186132361 1075 17592186132363 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186132361 1075 17592186132364 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186132361 1075 17592186132365 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186132361 1075 17592186132366 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186132361 1075 17592186551898 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1075 17592186552066 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1075 17592186552111 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1075 17592186552114 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1075 17592186552254 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186132361 1078 17592186551898 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133469 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3$." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133469 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple other than $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133470 1071 "Part of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown below. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\leq x \\leq 4$?\n\n![](images/59/59abf18dc978f48da4bec95bc702141b2459a327f194fda46d64f12c5bc8ffb4.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133470 1071 "Part of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown below. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\leq x \\leq 4?$\n\n![](images/59/59abf18dc978f48da4bec95bc702141b2459a327f194fda46d64f12c5bc8ffb4.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133470 1074 "The graph above shows the function in the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nSince this interval has length $2$, and the period of the function is also $2$, this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2$.\n\n![](images/41/41a583651bf690810335776b9e7b5620e6b6830e1b59ee9d646c4ab429e17de8.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133470 1074 "The graph above shows the function in the interval $0 \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\nSince this interval has length $2,$ and the period of the function is also $2,$ this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2.$\n\n![](images/41/41a583651bf690810335776b9e7b5620e6b6830e1b59ee9d646c4ab429e17de8.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133471 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Given that $f$ is periodic with period $4$, what is the value of $f(84)$?\n\n![](images/b7/b763649968dc1a117007eba0b7cb0862473aadf702e8c91fc30ac83149445865.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133471 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Given that $f$ is periodic with period $4$, what is the value of $f(84)?$\n\n![](images/b7/b763649968dc1a117007eba0b7cb0862473aadf702e8c91fc30ac83149445865.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133471 1074 "Since the period is $4$, if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(84) &= f(84 - 4) \\\\\n&= f(84 - 2 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= ⋯ \\\\\n&= f(84 - 20 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= f(4) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133471 1074 "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(84) & =f(84-4) \\\\  & =f(84-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(84-20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133536 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133536 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n) =\\dfrac{6}{(-2)^n}$ for $n\\geq 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133536 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (6)/((-2)^{1}) = (6)/((-2)) =-3, \\\\\nf(2) &= (6)/((-2)^{2}) = \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = (f(2))/(f(1)) = ((\\frac{3}{2}))/((-3)) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133536 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = r \\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{1}}=\\frac{6}{(-2)}=-3, \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(-3)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = -\\dfrac{1}{2} f(n), \\qquad f(1) = -3, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133537 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133537 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence\n$a_{n+1} = 3a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = 1,\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133537 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1$. To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3$.\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133537 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $a_1=1.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = 3.$\n\nSubstituting $a_1 = 1$ and $r = 3$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}\t\\[5pt]\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{n-1} \\[5pt]\n&= 3^{n-1}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=3^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133550 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133550 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $a_n = 8 \\cdot 3^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133550 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1$. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{1} = 8 \\cdot 3 = 24, \\\\\na_{2} &= 8 \\cdot 3^{2} = 8 \\cdot 9 = 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{72}{24} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133550 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r \\cdot a_n.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula,  we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $a_1.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =8\\cdot {3}^{1}=8\\cdot 3=24, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =8\\cdot {3}^{2}=8\\cdot 9=72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{72}{24}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric  sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3 a_n, \\quad a_1 = 24, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133551 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\nf(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133551 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n$f(n+1) =-\\dfrac{f(n)}{3},\\, \\quad f(1) = {\\color{blue}9},\\, \\quad n\\geq 1$?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133551 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=-\\frac{f(n)}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=9.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1) = 9$ and $r = -\\dfrac{1}{3}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}\t\\[5pt]\n&= 9 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}.\n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =9\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1})\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & =-27\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-3)^{n}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{(-3)^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n} ,\\quad n\\geq 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133551 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n + 1) = - (f(n))/(3) =-\\frac{1}{3}f(n)\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n) =f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\nf(1)=9\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = {\\color{red}r} \\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf(1) = 9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n) &= f(1) \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[5pt] &= 9 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{n-1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(n) &= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{-1} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{1}) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})^{n} \\\\\n&=-27 \\cdot (1)/((-3)^{n}) \\\\\n&= - (27)/((-3)^{n})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\dfrac{27}{(-3)^n},\\quad n\\geq 1\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133619 1071 "Let $q(x)=2x^3+3x^2-18x+8$. Given that $q(2) = 0$, factor $q(x)$ completely and identify all three of its roots." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133619 1071 "Let $q(x)=2x^3+3x^2-18x+8.$ Given that $q(2) = 0$, factor $q(x)$ completely and identify all three of its roots." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133619 1074 "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x)$. To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x)$.\n\nSo $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x)$, we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-18$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4)\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 4 &= 0 & 2x - 1|= 0 & x - 2|= 0 \\\\ x|=-4 & x|= \\frac{1}{2} & x|= 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133619 1074 "If $q(2) = 0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x).$ To find the remaining zeros, we need to find the other factors of $q(x).$\n\n So $(x-2)$ is a factor of $q(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $q(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $q(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-18$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $14$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3+3x^2-18x+8}{x-2} = 2x^2+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x^2+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n2x^3+3x^2-18x+8 = (x-2)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to $0$ and 