 (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4(1)(10) = -4 <           0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 6x + 10$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x^2 - 2x + 20 = (x+2)(x^2 - 6x + 10).\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133641 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2$, factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133641 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133641 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2)\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 > 0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2)\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0$. Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x)$, we would need to solve the equations\n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186133641 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2}{x+1} = x^2 - 2x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1) (x^2 - 2x -2).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = b^2 - 4ac = (-2)^2 - 4(1)(-2) = 12 >     0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $x^2 - 2x - 2$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\nx^3 - x^2 - 4x - 2 = (x+1)(x^2 - 2x - 2).\n$$\n\n**Important Note**: Although the quadratic polynomial $(x^2 - 2x - 2)$ does not factor over the rational numbers, it *does* have real roots, because $\\mathcal D > 0.$ Therefore, if we were asked to find all of the real roots of $f(x),$ we would need to solve the equations \n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x^2 - 2x - 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe quadratic equation could be solved using the quadratic formula or by completing the square." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186133808 1038 175921