ite down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](images/2d/2d7e7ed4884b98bd6258ed6e3752f9b6bf1e779041e769ca0e177f8b9b385a50.svg)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186161804 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](images/33/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186161846 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/50/501074b2c8aa951c9236dc628de2a564f31b21b5ac5b4dd34d7b1324d4c8a331.svg)\nIf $Y =1-3X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186161846 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y =1-3X$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186161846 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 < x < 1$, we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(1 - 3X \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(-3X \\le y - 1) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge \\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}(1 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{9}(1 - y),\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < y < 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186161846 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X,$ where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = 1-3X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0 < x < 1,$ we have $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1 .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bb641b1920d0ded33e4ffa25f3e0af856550dd3820edf30416bd79c3a6da264.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-3X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-3X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $-2 \\lt y \\lt 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\frac{1-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(1-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n![](images/b0/b00d02901c82c0544e8e576577832184919da2338cf05d2d8a3fa230d45993c4.svg)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186161847 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n![](images/33/3357fbadc12363114c7274b17df9f8e992566d53f8233841783338687b88989e.svg)\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186161847 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n${\\frac{3}{8}(2 - x)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nIf $Y = (2-X)^3$, then what is the probability density function of $Y$?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186161847 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y$, where $Y = u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X$, we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$.\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y = (2-X)^3$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $0 \\lt x \\lt 2$, we have $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$.\n\n![](images/2c/2c07ffe5af282ba1689cb6f49201b979afb4186d0128dd6a97f446b7240d76d6.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P((2 - X)^{3} \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2 - X \\le y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= P(-X \\le y^{1/3} - 2) \\\\\n&= P(X \\ge 2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= 1 - F_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $0 \\lt y \\lt 8$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 - (-\\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/3}} \\cdot f_{X}(2 - y^{1/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3y^{2/