*. The distribution function method consists of two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X$, where $F_Y$ and $F_X$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X$, respectively.\n- Then, we differentiate $F_Y$ to get the probability density function $f_Y$ of $Y$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll assume that the function $u$ is strictly monotonic (i.e., strictly increasing or decreasing). The case where $u$ is not monotonic will be dealt with in a future lesson.\n\nLet's see an example of the distribution function method in action." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186163339 1064 "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n![](images/88/88103878042469595fc8f8243958b44b128c0d38465bfe93a31f83afd6988300.svg)\nLet $Y= 1+2X.$ Let's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nFirst, note that $Y = 1+2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x > 0,$ we have $y > 1.$\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-(y-1)/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & \\,y>1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186163339 1064 "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n${e^{-x},\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nLet\n\n$$\nY= 1+2X\n$$\n\nLet's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y$.\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\nY = 1+2X\n$$\n\nis a strictly increasing function of $X$. Moreover, for $x > 0$, we have $y > 1$.\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1$. We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y$, and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= P(Y \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2X + 1 \\le y) \\\\\n&= P(2X \\le y - 1) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le \\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= F_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1$, we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= F_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(F_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot F_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot f_{X}(\\frac{y - 1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot e^{- (y - 1)/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}e^{(1 - y)/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= {\\frac{1}{2}e^{(1 - y)/2},\\begin{vmatrix}y > 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186164198 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186164199 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186164200 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186164201 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186164202 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186164197 1075 17592186551673 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186164197 1075 17592186551810 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186164197 1075 17592186551844 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186164197 1075 17592186551851 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186164197 1075 17592186552229 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186164197 1078 17592186551673 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 17592186552459 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186167196 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186167196 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the s