 }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6(3-(-3)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-36)=-144.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186181416 1078 17592186181421 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186181475 1075 17592186551825 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186181475 1078 17592186551825 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184143 1038 17592186552427 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184143 1038 17592186552438 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184143 1038 17592186552460 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184143 1038 17592186552481 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184165 1038 17592186552461 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184165 1038 17592186552501 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184166 1038 17592186552491 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184166 1038 17592186552506 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184229 1038 17592186552407 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184229 1038 17592186552416 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184229 1038 17592186552429 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184229 1038 17592186552478 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184424 1050 660.0 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184540 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184540 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u =-\\frac{24}{25}, \\cos u =-\\frac{7}{25}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186184540 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184540 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{125}-\\frac{72}{125} \\\\  & =-\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184552 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184552 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 4x^{2} + 5$ for $x \\le 0$.$f^{-1}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186184552 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+5$ for $x\\le 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186184552 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=4{x}^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{y}^{2}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =4{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{\\frac{x-5}{4}} & =y \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x-5}}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x-5}}{2}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186185350 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}u & =1-{sin}^{2}u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply 