10b1ba9bef5cc6c434755e74196d1829a30f2c4a3c53.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{8x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186372621 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186372621 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 false] [17592186372621 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(7\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t+28\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡28\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡28\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186372621 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(7\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t+28\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡28\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡28\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s