on $D$ onto the $yz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type II region enclosed between the back surface $x={u}_{1}(y,z)=1$ and the front surface $x={u}_{2}(y,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/e5/e5779dc92c875bef280425ed24a17cfb53f9f2a9736292a92ccdb988044237a6.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $yz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\nz=1-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n![](images/00/00f2847a8e4c927a1c6631f4aef03c57b9d28f18341b268182501ada07225382.png)\n\nHence, $D=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z[x]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1068 17592186552369 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1069 "q-343282" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+1)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=6.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1072 #uuid "0445e2f8-6c9c-5e30-8a27-7e22ee9c426c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552402 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1068 17592186552344 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1069 "q-110111" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\, & -1<x<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=10X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1072 #uuid "09cc9808-be42-54c9-b4a2-69b0d9a20cf1" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552403 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=10X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $-1<x<0,$ we have $-10<y<0.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b03f4d44db5b0db0498469f80fa25e56177a8602c657d8506105837212bf5ba.png)\n\n*Note: Image not drawn to scale.*\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $-10<y<0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(10X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 0.1y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(0.1y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $-10<y<0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(0.1y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot {f}_{X}(0.1y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =0.1\\cdot {f}_{X}(0.1y) \\\\  & =0.1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0.1y \\\\  & =-0.02y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-0.02y, & -10<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1068 17592186552316 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1069 "q-248223" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1071 "A newspaper in a town has $100\\,000$ subscribers, and $56\\,000$ of them have a digital subscription. If the newspaper randomly samples $224$ subscribers, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $112$ and $140$ subscribers (inclusive) in the sample have a digital subscription. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0359\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0351\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0344\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9616\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9693\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9756\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1072 #uuid "0c142fbc-f51c-5174-b26c-467ddea21c93" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552404 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=224,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{56\\,000}{100\\,000}=0.56.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=224(0.56)=125.44>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=224(1-0.56)=98.56>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=224$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,\\frac{0.56(1-0.56)}{224}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,0.0011).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $112$ and $140$ of subscribers in the sample have a digital subscription, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{112}{224}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{140}{224} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.625-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.81\\le Z\\le 1.96) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.96)-P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.96)=0.9750,\\,Φ(-1.81)=0.0351.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.9750-0.0351 \\\\  & =0.9399.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1068 17592186552309 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1069 "q-140552" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1072 #uuid "0f7fea28-dc13-51b3-b3d3-9108cf056cc8" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552405 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1068 17592186552386 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1069 "q-154703" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1071 "If $|3-\\frac{2}{3}y|=5,$ then $y=$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1072 #uuid "0fd09d79-cd92-571a-b908-4f0df92196ae" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552406 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|3-\\frac{2}{3}y|=3-\\frac{2}{3}y,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-\\frac{2}{3}y| & =5 \\\\ 3-\\frac{2}{3}y & =5 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $-\\frac{3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}y & =2 \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}y) & =(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (2) \\\\ (\\cancel{-\\frac{3}{2}})\\cdot (\\cancel{-\\frac{2}{3}}y) & =-\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ y & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|3-\\frac{2}{3}y|=-(3-\\frac{2}{3}y),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-\\frac{2}{3}y| & =5 \\\\ -(3-\\frac{2}{3}y) & =5 \\\\ -3+\\frac{2}{3}y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $\\frac{3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y & =8 \\\\ (\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}y) & =(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (8) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{3}{2}})\\cdot (\\cancel{\\frac{2}{3}}y) & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\ y & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=12.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1068 17592186552283 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1068 17592186552292 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1069 "q-325842" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that both $u$ and $v$ lie in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{3}\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1072 #uuid "1059df0b-e279-5183-9a0b-f6c9c0f186a7" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552407 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50}+\\frac{7}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}+7}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1068 17592186552275 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1068 17592186552303 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1069 "q-251874" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is ${a}_{n}=5\\cdot {4}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the recursive formula for this sequence? Express your answer in terms of ${a}_{n}.$\n\n${a}_{n+1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{a}_{1}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1072 #uuid "1246524a-95f9-5d04-bcaf-769f77cf931a" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552408 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term ${a}_{1}.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5\\cdot {4}^{1}=5\\cdot 4=20, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =5\\cdot {4}^{2}=5\\cdot 16=80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{80}{20}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=20,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1068 17592186552267 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1069 "q-7661" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24.$ Given that $f(4)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1072 #uuid "148f1fdf-ffa7-5ddd-8683-94b6614f6d0f" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552409 1074 "If $f(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $34$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $8$ | $-28$ | $24$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-7$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24}{x-4}=2{x}^{2}-7x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+34x-24=(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)(2{x}^{2}-7x+6) & =(x-4)(2x-3)(x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-3)(x-2)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1068 17592186552269 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1069 "q-139862" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$25$** | **$20$** | **$15$** | **$12$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$12$** | **$20$** | **$30$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.  Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1072 #uuid "1692117f-e477-5d47-ac2a-92ef20a7f5cf" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552410 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$25$** | **$20$** | **$15$** | **$12$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$12$** | **$20$** | **$30$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 30 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 20 & 12 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 15 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 12 & 30\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1068 17592186552326 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1069 "q-130861" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1072 #uuid "18af1f73-e41b-5d4c-86dd-e0a8cf91a4f0" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552411 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same  elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1068 17592186552272 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1069 "q-144920" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}(2-x), & -3<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y={X}^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<1 \\\\ h(y), & 1\\le y<9 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1072 #uuid "1a1ee509-91e0-56a1-9d72-7806568cd6ce" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552412 1074 "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y={X}^{2}$ over the interval $-3<x<1.$\n\n![](images/73/7332c58097d1a2933c10152028bc715a147e820ae14b2fae92fe288eaf956ce0.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $-3<x\\le -1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $1\\le y<9.$\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $-1<x<1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<1.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<1.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{1}{12}(2-\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{12}(2+\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1068 17592186552397 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1069 "q-18153" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1072 #uuid "1adb95f2-1de2-5cd2-b372-b57ff2f582da" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552413 1074 "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & 1 & -25 & 12 \\\\  &  & 6 & 21 & -12 \\\\  & 2 & 7 & -4 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12}{x-3}=2{x}^{2}+7x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-25x+12=(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+7x-4)=(x-3)(2x-1)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-4=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1068 17592186552354 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1069 "q-124430" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1071 "![](images/e9/e9610ceabe4a1e82995e589f482887dd9f4325218cfb5d37b5795dcb5a3bbbdc.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{9}{8},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1072 #uuid "1bc85b39-7b87-56e7-bb5c-212278f36ea1" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552414 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{9}{8}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\frac{9}{8} & =\\frac{4x-2}{3x+1} \\\\ 9(3x+1) & =8(4x-2) \\\\ 27x+9 & =32x-16 \\\\ 9+16 & =32x-27x \\\\ 25 & =5x \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =4x-2=4(5)-2=18, \\\\ AC & =3x+1=3(5)+1=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1068 17592186552270 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1069 "q-244482" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1071 "![](images/ed/ed4fb3bd21624b15349fd520c2944389d81af99d81962fa54b815628fd7360aa.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to one decimal place, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1072 #uuid "1eaad09e-a27c-5525-b0d1-ca7f3b1ec2af" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552415 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠B={35}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠B,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({35}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x+2}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({35}^{∘}) & =x+2 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({35}^{∘})-x & =2 \\\\ x(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({35}^{∘})-1) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({35}^{∘})-1} \\\\ x & =4.994\\,8…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x=2(4.994\\,8…)=9.989\\,6…, \\\\ AC & =x+2=(4.994\\,8…)+2=6.994\\,8….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(9.989\\,6…)(6.994\\,8…) \\\\  & =34.937\\,8… \\\\  & ≈34.9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1068 17592186552296 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1069 "q-119581" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}-b}{26},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1072 #uuid "1ed725f0-66d2-5a2d-ad80-26e42df0423f" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552416 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}-5}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12,b=5,$ and $a+b=17.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1068 17592186552337 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1069 "q-110126" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=1-{X}^{5},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1072 #uuid "1f799a56-7472-58ef-998d-84e22fb88853" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552417 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=1-{X}^{5}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/05/05534475aecc1d0ebad2234f1259d1b6fd1c6183e47ad763f17c87c5fdb06afb.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-{X}^{5}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-{X}^{5}\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge (1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot {f}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot {f}_{X}((1-y)^{1/5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(1-y)^{4/5}}\\cdot 5{((1-y)^{1/5})}^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1068 17592186552350 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1069 "q-139846" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$15$** | **$22$** | **$28$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $14$ | $24$ |\n\n \n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1072 #uuid "2059b1c7-de38-50f0-b3b0-9bc3855a6603" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552418 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$15$** | **$22$** | **$28$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $14$ | $24$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 28\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1068 17592186552279 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1069 "q-102338" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1072 #uuid "226b9b94-926b-577f-834a-35ff659d1159" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552419 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=-3.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1068 17592186552335 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1069 "q-300754" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1072 #uuid "23394483-7f6f-560b-bb8e-2bc28d2cef6c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552420 1074 "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -5}\\frac{4x}{x+1} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -5}4x}{{lim}_{x\\to -5}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-5)}{-5+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{-4} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1068 17592186552351 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1069 "q-97997" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1072 #uuid "24473d8c-fe55-5d76-b96c-10c4724ef4f1" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552421 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1068 17592186552362 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1069 "q-130840" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1072 #uuid "262002ef-4fd0-5419-8127-789c5234e1d0" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552422 1074 "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $-9$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-9{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1068 17592186552324 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1068 17592186552363 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1068 17592186552377 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1069 "q-326315" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1071 "A polynomial $g(x)$ is defined as $g(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4.$ Given that $g(4)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $g(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1072 #uuid "26aef9f4-359f-5b52-aa4f-51a7c5f79b8e" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552423 1074 "If $g(4)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $g(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $g(x).$\n\nSince $(x-4)$ is a factor of $g(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $g(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $g(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-4)({x}^{2}-1)=(x-4)(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=1,\\text{ and}\\,x=4.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1068 17592186552257 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1069 "q-39370" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3.$ Given that $f(3)=0$, what is the sum of the roots of the polynomial?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1072 #uuid "349d9aca-faa9-56e3-8806-6dcea184619f" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552424 1074 "If $f(3)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 4 & -12 & -1 & 3 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & -1 & \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3}{x-3}=4{x}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-x+3=(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(4{x}^{2}-1)=(x-3)(2x-1)(2x+1).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial are\n\n$$\nx=3,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots is\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1068 17592186552298 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1069 "q-110106" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=1-\\frac{1}{3}X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1072 #uuid "37fc4f1c-699a-5034-b164-6cf41c6e46b4" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552425 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=1-\\frac{1}{3}X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0<x<3,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/af/afe10720ce51bc3dd1b304ea276568d0e505f90159359f5ae54462e4caa729e7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(1-\\frac{X}{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le 3y-3) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge 3-3y) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 3-3y) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(3-3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-3)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3-3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(3-3y)^{2} \\\\  & =3{(1-y)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{(1-y)}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1068 17592186552342 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1069 "q-147484" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1071 "A call center manager knows that $40%$ of the calls are complaints. If the manager randomly samples $80$ calls, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ of them are complaints. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1072 #uuid "3b794abe-e844-5b01-84a2-a65b6d920bc6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552426 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.4.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.4)=32>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.4)=48>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,0.0030).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ calls are complaints, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{80}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{36}{80} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.45) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}<Z<\\frac{0.45-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<0.91) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.91)-P(Z<0.00) \\\\  & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0.00)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45) & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1068 17592186552330 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1069 "q-141440" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{7}-b\\sqrt{2}}{39},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1072 #uuid "3f0420a1-666b-5191-842f-3d2bdb44e681" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552427 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{39}+\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{39} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}-12\\sqrt{2}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=12,$ and $a+b=17.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1068 17592186552301 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1069 "q-3894" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${v}^{4}-4{v}^{3}+7v-28?$\n\nI. $v=-4$\n\nII. $v=4$\n\nIII. $v=2$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1072 #uuid "4251c35b-5737-5f81-8168-9bb04b9bfceb" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552428 1074 "- Substituting $-4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-4(-4)^{3}+7(-4)-28 & = \\\\ 256+256-28-28 & = \\\\ 456\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{4}-4(4)^{3}+7(4)-28 & = \\\\ 256-256+28-28 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $4$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+7(2)-28 & = \\\\ 16-32+14-28 & = \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the root is $v=4,$ so the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1068 17592186552358 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1069 "q-119592" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{2}-b\\sqrt{14}}{8},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1072 #uuid "43bdc32f-8f5e-5fac-84f7-776394b1cc9e" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552429 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8}+\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-1\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1068 17592186552374 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1069 "q-127075" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+(2+x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1072 #uuid "4696ec15-27d3-55f9-a25e-60bb43e6e055" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552430 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1068 17592186552319 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1069 "q-41412" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1071 "![](images/36/36e665c9de31f820a3ce60caea7f596198341cae67a60029a850f7a0a3403574.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1072 #uuid "46b9cd99-164f-5948-814f-d29d48209d6c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552431 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $2$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $2$ (or a multiple of $2$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $2.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1068 17592186552372 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1069 "q-75114" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1072 #uuid "46fec928-7e8c-5465-b67b-b3ef68641ebc" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552432 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the  three given elementary row operations. \n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1068 17592186552304 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1069 "q-269489" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1072 #uuid "47594296-da7d-5903-b008-c5f5265a8817" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552433 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 34\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1068 17592186552313 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1068 17592186552355 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1068 17592186552376 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1069 "q-269983" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, the roots of the polynomial $f(x),$ arranged in **ascending order**, are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,$ and\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1072 #uuid "47c5c0f9-0372-541a-ab67-517cecd45015" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552434 1074 "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8)=(x-1)(x-2)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=1,\\,x=2.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1068 17592186552370 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1069 "q-3896" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${u}^{4}-26{u}^{2}+25?$\n\nI. $u=1$\n\nII. $u=5$\n\nIII. $u=3$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1072 #uuid "492f172a-6ec8-53da-b873-64a863435b9b" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552435 1074 "- Substituting $1$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-26(1)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 1-26(1)+25 & = \\\\ 1-26+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $1$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $1$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)^{4}-26(5)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 625-26(25)+25 & = \\\\ 625-650+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $5$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $3$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{4}-26(3)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 81-26(9)+25 & = \\\\ 81-234+25 & = \\\\ -128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $3$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $u=1$ and $u=5,$ so the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1068 17592186552366 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1069 "q-343270" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(-1)=6.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1072 #uuid "4b516dd7-0e0d-5e63-914a-7e40f288e42a" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552436 1074 "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(-1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(-1) & =C{e}^{-(-1)} \\\\ 6 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =6{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-2}=6{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1068 17592186552328 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1069 "q-331045" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1072 #uuid "4d554be3-9b5d-5bb1-8354-199346e2e327" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552437 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+5) & =cos\\,(5\\pi (t+5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t+25\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡25\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡25\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+5)\\} & =L\\{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-5)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-5s}\\,L\\{g(t+5)\\} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1068 17592186552322 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1069 "q-141443" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}+b\\sqrt{5}}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1072 #uuid "50ab1b1c-89b6-5bde-a50d-34d9fce11246" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552438 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})-(\\frac{1}{4})(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{20}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{5}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=2,$ and $a+b=7.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1068 17592186552265 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1069 "q-131466" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1072 #uuid "51d9f041-304f-5c22-9ed6-3b3659033e6d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552439 1074 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3})=5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nFrom the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=0,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}(5f(x)+6g(x)+3{x}^{3}) & =5{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)+6{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)+3{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)^{3} \\\\  & =0+6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1068 17592186552338 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1069 "q-42811" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\,$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1072 #uuid "59ac31ae-d95c-5ade-b172-6fbd672350bd" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552440 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}$  to $A.$ \n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1068 17592186552336 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1068 17592186552353 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1069 "q-131464" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1072 #uuid "5b9c1b56-216e-5690-b417-9acbe40876e5" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552441 1074 "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $-1$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$  the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1068 17592186552391 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1069 "q-73268" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=13,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1072 #uuid "5e4e0f75-9894-567b-a1a8-c012ad934066" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552442 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=13.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=13$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =78\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1068 17592186552398 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1069 "q-42760" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1072 #uuid "608d80e2-812d-50df-95f4-906e16812c70" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552443 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1068 17592186552359 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1069 "q-74365" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1071 "For the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nII. The domain of the function is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nIII. The function is a decreasing function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1072 #uuid "61610231-8c8c-5bd5-b565-7a6368e4a10f" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552444 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5ed500c81d09e2775f09fd10a221d7ec0fca7e39249b7add7a680328b68c1.png)\n\nNow, we analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $y\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is defined on all real numbers except $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is an increasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1068 17592186552375 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1069 "q-342636" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1072 #uuid "669dd3eb-164c-527e-a5e3-447099f637bc" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552445 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(2(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-3)sin\\,(2(t-3))\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1068 17592186552285 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1069 "q-110124" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{4},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1072 #uuid "670a140c-55d4-5114-b612-9d3a283512c3" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552446 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note  that since the possible values of $X$ are $0<x<1,$ $Y={X}^{4}$ is a strictly increasing function of $X$ when $0<x<1.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a6f01a3538711e45e581038af5eaf95e229bf72d08bafefbcf34786a40c1d2d.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{4}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}({y}^{1/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{1/4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{y}^{3/4}}\\cdot 2({y}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1068 17592186552268 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1069 "q-131465" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}Cf(x)=8,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1072 #uuid "684140b2-affb-5ccb-83ee-320705bb77fa" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552447 1074 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=-1.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}Cf(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =8 \\\\ C\\cdot (-1) & =8 \\\\ C & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1068 17592186552299 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1069 "q-161592" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1071 "![](images/f2/f2f91e4ee1a90e1afb00d2f212e057235afe5c4e9089679da3af244c1d324676.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}12x\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1072 #uuid "688ce6d7-73b6-5ba5-87a1-28b0bc9bdadb" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552448 1074 "![](images/b7/b7e71e46310b065e2c03b4a0aebaa96268136c7f633eea6983612297e56361d1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/14/145f0d494ffe24de84e3a289b3a7316d3f4933f193d734aed118609f7fb9f94f.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}12x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}12x\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}12x\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}12x\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6(1+y+z)^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6+6{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2}+12yz+12y+12z)\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6+6{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2}+12yz+12y+12z)\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6+6{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2}+12yz+12y+12z)\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6z+6{y}^{2}z+2{z}^{3}+6y{z}^{2}+12yz+6{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6+6{y}^{2}+2+6y+12y+6)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+18y+14)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+18y+14)\\,dy & =[2{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2}+14y]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(2+9+14)-0 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1068 17592186552307 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1069 "q-146954" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population where $30%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $40%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1072 #uuid "690b1c72-fb42-5766-9459-80c219f09698" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552449 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1068 17592186552278 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1069 "q-75076" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1072 #uuid "697a3132-99c3-5286-b927-185afe5caf9e" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552450 1074 "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $6$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1068 17592186552294 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1069 "q-41476" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1071 "![](images/0c/0ca6c9e91c17cea351f60cc475f0feab876a29a4877114c06ce9408863a8f7dc.png)\n\nPart of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown above. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\le x\\le 4?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1072 #uuid "69d97a06-9ba7-540b-b234-776ced61d231" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552451 1074 "The graph above shows the function in the interval $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\nSince this interval has length $2,$ and the period of the function is also $2,$ this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc628e23b36aa95aa47e42c6bae14caf29651c46abcea1bf26408b44f2660f10.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1068 17592186552332 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1069 "q-331027" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1071 "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1072 #uuid "6c235044-2675-5c70-a69d-3ac1bca6c5ef" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552452 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)({t}^{2}+6t+2)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+6t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =(t+4)^{2}+6(t+4)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+8t+16\\,+\\,6t+24\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+14t+42\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+14L\\{t\\}+42L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+42\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{42}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1068 17592186552334 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1069 "q-32010" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1071 "Let $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1072 #uuid "6f2a6c54-ca8b-593a-b61e-5ecaccecd8e7" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552453 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of the identity matrix by $-2$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation  ${R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1068 17592186552389 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1069 "q-95060" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=15.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1072 #uuid "71a50d5a-430c-5d52-842d-aa1bb8a09b41" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552454 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=15\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1068 17592186552325 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1069 "q-144343" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{x}^{2}, & -2<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2}+1,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1072 #uuid "738870fc-587d-5975-8073-552a681e8d2c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552455 1074 "First, notice that the transformation $Y={X}^{2}+1$ is two-to-one over the entire interval $-2<x<2.$ \n\n![](images/8a/8a03bd46aa6d415d8e1a49ff74557886702c1dc4bc9164ce2a5a1252874e1b4e.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-2<x<2,$  we have that $Y$ is nonzero on $1\\le y<5.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-(y-1)^{1/2}\\le X\\le (y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[\\frac{3}{16}{((y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{(-(y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}(\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)+\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1068 17592186552308 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1069 "q-112540" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1072 #uuid "73e5f0e5-34ff-58f3-baa3-f951a9265d77" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552456 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={1}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+x+3$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1068 17592186552302 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1069 "q-342627" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1071 "Find $L\\{u(t-1){e}^{-6(t-1)}\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1072 #uuid "7459efdc-2436-5537-949c-53e76f0f388b" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552457 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1){e}^{-6(t-1)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1)={e}^{-6(t-1)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-6t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{{e}^{-6t}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-(-6)}=\\frac{1}{s+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1){e}^{-6(t-1)}\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1068 17592186552341 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1069 "q-110343" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt[3]{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1072 #uuid "75b7b0a4-c7ee-525c-b9ca-1a7df7652ed9" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552458 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=-\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y<0.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2b1503e6624c9060829468c9fa0d49856a2d76131975b6b1e437b7de3285c7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y<0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt[3]{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-X\\le {y}^{3}) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge -{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(-{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-3{y}^{2})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-{y}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4(-{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{2}{e}^{4{y}^{3}},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1068 17592186552276 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1068 17592186552340 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1068 17592186552365 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1069 "q-336705" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into $\\text{L1}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1072 #uuid "77948b35-77f3-58cd-80e2-2c25a0aff174" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552459 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1068 17592186552383 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1069 "q-141441" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=-\\frac{a\\sqrt{2}+b}{14},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1072 #uuid "77e70b3f-8704-511e-87c9-d67cfcbe9ec9" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552460 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{7})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}+5}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=5,$ and $a+b=11.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1068 17592186552345 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1069 "q-143082" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-6x+11$ for $x\\ge 3.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1072 #uuid "78fb1a6e-17a5-5dc5-a34e-f2b26dac2b07" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552461 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-6x+11 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+11 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}-9+11 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=(x-3)^{2}+2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y-3)^{2}+2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y-3)^{2}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =(y-3)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & =y-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+3 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 3$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 3.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}+3\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1068 17592186552347 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1069 "q-131467" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1072 #uuid "7b122753-4256-5b6b-bc1d-003f930bcebd" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552462 1074 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=1.$ It is impossible to draw the function in a neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1068 17592186552331 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1069 "q-151981" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{3},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1072 #uuid "7be864d2-62c5-5172-87fe-bf187923ab8c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552463 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=\\frac{X}{3}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n![](images/42/42a3c2510927b66842ec0360eabfb169a306ccaf52292132f31769036e608e69.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{X}{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-(3y)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1068 17592186552291 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1068 17592186552352 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1069 "q-131461" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1072 #uuid "825d48d1-753b-5c3d-88ac-771966b26aa9" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552464 1074 "As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1068 17592186552300 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1069 "q-127052" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=3xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1072 #uuid "832871bc-122c-548e-b34d-eed1097a268c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552465 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(3xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $2{x}^{2}y.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1068 17592186552295 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1069 "q-140512" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1071 "![](images/6f/6f878dc844ba17668a2298c38a47b3af5684acedbae6e2c0934c396f9cbff0a5.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1072 #uuid "84285f76-9289-52c2-aa2b-3324df33ee37" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552466 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/aa/aaa23fd66546dbeb138c1dca851a2eb826a892edbde4cc7b5efafa29d6195580.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1068 17592186552315 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1069 "q-151985" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3},\\, & 1<x<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1072 #uuid "8460a557-83b6-5efd-a8a5-c94e63881b6d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552467 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $1<x<2,$ we have $2<y<4.$\n\n![](images/60/60342277a025cb26cebab2ac534fad4aa1b49fc2285571c303c4e829eb464606.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $2<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $2<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2(\\frac{y}{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1068 17592186552357 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1069 "q-159735" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$31$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $9$ | $13$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1072 #uuid "85645ca9-c255-514c-85e9-8b4da9dc59e6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552468 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$31$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $9$ | $13$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 19 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ 4 & 31 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1068 17592186552297 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1069 "q-144913" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & 0<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(y), & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1072 #uuid "858574ee-13d7-5994-a0b6-afd2faa00bae" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552469 1074 "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ over the interval $0<x<4.$\n\n![](images/16/163ffe5a4c5bcbce95369f8082c8dbfababe7c40315d8136d79c9e370f05a1a1.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $0<x<2.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $2\\le x<4.$ On this interval,  $Y$ is nonzero on  $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X-1|\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(-2y\\le X-1\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(1-2y\\le X\\le 2y+1) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(2y+1)-{F}_{X}(1-2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{2},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =2{F}_{X}^{′}(2y+1)+2{F}_{X}^{′}(1-2y) \\\\  & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1)+2{f}_{X}(1-2y) \\\\  & =2({f}_{X}(2y+1)+{f}_{X}(1-2y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2({f}_{X}(2y+1)+{f}_{X}(1-2y)) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{8}(2y+1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1-2y)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1068 17592186552385 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1069 "q-127054" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1071 "![](images/0d/0dd48f19e9940f1684c0cbb723e772384dda3d7429fc89c2c9dc528f7ebfe42c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1072 #uuid "89cfc1e4-7ca7-548a-8985-27acedeb025d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552470 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{4}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-j.$\n\n![](images/c5/c591fedf1b1c8ea705f0c13fbfd96bc7f1f6701841e3eff03bd0578aa2f8ab06.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}y.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1068 17592186552290 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1069 "q-67547" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1071 "![](images/8a/8af022dcc3065a036ba04fb79e75c43906ccef7f32e3416917778afec3d344a8.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{7}{3},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1072 #uuid "8a507b16-48a7-5e03-967f-cb9296e5eda5" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552471 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{4x-1}{x+1} \\\\ 7(x+1) & =3(4x-1) \\\\ 7x+7 & =12x-3 \\\\ 7x-12x & =-3-7 \\\\ -5x & =-10 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =x+1=2+1=3, \\\\ BC & =4x-1=4(2)-1=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1068 17592186552263 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1068 17592186552314 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1069 "q-215127" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1071 "Written in **ascending order**, the solutions to $|5y-1|=6$ are $y=${{field-1}} and $y=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1072 #uuid "8bfe078d-4df3-523f-8453-0c2505e6812e" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552472 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|5y-1|=5y-1,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5y-1| & =6 \\\\ 5y-1 & =6 \\\\ 5y & =7 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{7}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|5y-1|=-(5y-1),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5y-1| & =6 \\\\ -(5y-1) & =6 \\\\ -5y+1 & =6 \\\\ -5y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & =\\frac{5}{-5} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1068 17592186552261 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1069 "q-165279" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1071 "![](images/93/93d069bfebd0d91cac459285a0e92cd14b5eab90bc465d7728b80b4f6fcbd47a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $5,$ what is the value of $f(-37)?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1072 #uuid "8e32adf3-1916-555c-a43d-93229e1ec567" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552473 1074 "Since the period is $5,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $5$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep adding $5$ to the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-37) & =f(-37+5) \\\\  & =f(-37+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(-37+8\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =f(3) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1068 17592186552281 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1069 "q-112551" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1071 "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1072 #uuid "8fccdf12-2a25-54ff-895d-1b990f2006e3" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552474 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $13$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(1)=5>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x+1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+13x-4=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+1).\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1068 17592186552288 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1069 "q-112200" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1071 "If $|8-2x|=5,$ then $x=$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1072 #uuid "8ff91a7b-d943-550d-955e-e036467fba09" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552475 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|8-2x|=8-2x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|8-2x| & =5 \\\\ 8-2x & =5 \\\\ -2x & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2} & =\\frac{-3}{-2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|8-2x|=-(8-2x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|8-2x| & =5 \\\\ -(8-2x) & =5 \\\\ -8+2x & =5 \\\\ 2x & =13 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{13}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{2}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1068 17592186552312 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1069 "q-269476" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1072 #uuid "9669b6bb-a27d-584f-935a-b35adacb11c6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552476 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1068 17592186552367 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1069 "q-144034" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & 0<x<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=(X-3)^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<4 \\\\ h(y), & 4\\le y<9 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1072 #uuid "97ae858a-243f-5feb-afeb-22f3b7dc533e" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552477 1074 "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=(X-3)^{2}$ over the interval $0<x<5.$\n\n![](images/6b/6b75d7be28fd4ec38257d1a8899960f38ce616e504e02f5f371620509afe81ac.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $0<x\\le 1.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $4\\le y<9.$\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $1<x<5.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<4.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<4.$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((X-3)^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X-3\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(3-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le 3+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(3+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(3-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{y}}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1068 17592186552348 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1069 "q-143842" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1072 #uuid "97e789b8-dcb1-527a-b969-3a0ccc1e1111" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552478 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{15}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1068 17592186552320 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1069 "q-44987" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{7},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1072 #uuid "98925317-9d30-5e69-bd45-e05c5755cffe" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552479 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=3.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}) \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{1}{{7}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1068 17592186552282 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1069 "q-101752" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1072 #uuid "9a2f8eb3-e0d7-5d85-8fb5-62ab15cba80d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552480 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{5\\cdot 1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1068 17592186552361 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1069 "q-325885" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1072 #uuid "9e4e39a8-9ee3-514c-8a99-c8c4e6788313" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552481 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{26}-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{26} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1068 17592186552382 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1069 "q-331018" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1071 "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L\\{u(t-7)({t}^{2}+2)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1072 #uuid "9fe4c9be-d1a0-58fd-a27b-7c4761a3ccb5" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552482 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-7)({t}^{2}+2)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=7,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-7)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-7s}\\,L\\{g(t+7)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+7) & =(t+7)^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+49\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+14t+51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+7)\\} & =L\\{{t}^{2}+14t+51\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+14L\\{t\\}+51L\\{1\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+14\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+51\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-7)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-7s}\\,L\\{g(t+7)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{51}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1068 17592186552318 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1069 "q-109880" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=4,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1072 #uuid "a040fd0d-33b9-5cd2-ba84-4a64f3c5606d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552483 1074 "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=4.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=4$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1068 17592186552256 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1069 "q-144270" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & 0<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(y), & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $h(y).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1072 #uuid "a04f88da-8653-5b9a-90b7-8404ed30a5a3" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552484 1074 "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|$ over the interval $0<x<4.$\n\n![](images/16/163ffe5a4c5bcbce95369f8082c8dbfababe7c40315d8136d79c9e370f05a1a1.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $0<x<2.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $2\\le x<4.$ On this interval,  $Y$ is nonzero on  $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{2}|X-1|\\le y) \\\\  & =P(|X-1|\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(X-1\\le 2y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2y+1) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $|X-1|=X-1$ for $X>1.$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le y<\\frac{3}{2},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =2{F}_{X}^{′}(2y+1) \\\\  & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2{f}_{X}(2y+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}(2y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(2y+1).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1068 17592186552310 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1069 "q-97895" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A-I)={R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1072 #uuid "a1882648-2096-59b1-b0c6-167402b1eeb7" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552485 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1068 17592186552396 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1069 "q-110245" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1072 #uuid "a4b19843-0372-523c-be3f-e38cf328fccc" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552486 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that since the values of $X$ are $0<x<1,$ $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a2a6f4c5e26819af509e9347fb7aff230927bdd9192ad10a48ce9c951c7be80.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\ge -y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge {e}^{-y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}({e}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{-y})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1068 17592186552280 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1069 "q-94998" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=6?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1072 #uuid "a50d3b17-83c9-5e15-b240-68b23f1a29a6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552487 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $2{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1068 17592186552387 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1069 "q-75252" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1071 "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=9-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1072 #uuid "a55a479e-4a45-5971-8018-f156cd1ee741" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552488 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/97/97220aa0d23d01e807608e6194a37661ba77cc391d0adf537908c1f13b1b6de8.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/30/306666a0e69fca9dc47c2295aa616a944482f00b28fdd96d9d96af816b058447.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/15/15512c5f5063309474534a54b323d87c28cf9b776c10789a8f70e12cdb898aeb.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,3)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,5)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,5)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0=32.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1068 17592186552277 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1068 17592186552384 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1068 17592186552394 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1069 "q-276048" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1072 #uuid "a5918743-e3c0-5f81-afbc-29edc571e474" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552489 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-3 \\\\ -3+0+2 \\\\ -3+0+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1068 17592186552286 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1069 "q-327191" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8,$ factor $f(x)$ over the real numbers.\n\n$f(x)=(x+1)\\cdot ($ \n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1072 #uuid "ad3c509d-b392-55c9-8bbd-386b08676ca2" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552490 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8}{x+1}={x}^{2}-2x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={2}^{2}-4(1)(8)=-28<0.\n$$\n\nSo the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-2x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+6x+8=(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+8).\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1068 17592186552392 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1069 "q-143064" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}$ for $x\\le 2.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1072 #uuid "b20bccf7-d7c5-5c00-be58-d20d6291b56b" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552491 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x-2)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-(x-2)^{2}+4\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=-(y-2)^{2}+4\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-(y-2)^{2}+4 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4-x \\\\ y-2 & =-\\sqrt{4-x} \\\\ y & =2-\\sqrt{4-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the negative square root because if the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\le 2,$ then the range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is  ${f}^{-1}\\le 2.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\sqrt{4-x}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1068 17592186552266 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1069 "q-127061" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1072 #uuid "b2316759-1f4d-59b3-aebb-24c5b1ac8267" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552492 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1068 17592186552364 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1069 "q-319797" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1071 "What is the equation of the *horizontal* asymptote of the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1072 #uuid "b45e817e-9efe-5710-bdbb-65f233f4a487" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552493 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/40/400dad20519cecf16db43999fd18787643040023135f9e45b0b9f81a98d3717f.png)\n\nNotice that $y\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1068 17592186552333 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1069 "q-73990" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1071 "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2,$ factor $f(x)$ over the rational numbers." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1072 #uuid "b5caf723-bcce-5d2b-851d-852d72f2df0c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552494 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2}{x-2}={x}^{2}-3x-1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2=(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x-1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(1)(-1)=13>0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}-3x-1$ cannot be factored over the rational numbers since its discriminant is not a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ (over the rational numbers) is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+5x+2=(x-2)({x}^{2}-3x-1).\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1068 17592186552262 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1069 "q-131463" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1072 #uuid "b69f893c-224f-5a62-ac7c-a87ffc7746ab" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552495 1074 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=-1$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=-1$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1068 17592186552343 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1069 "q-31997" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A.$ Find the equivalent notation in terms of matrix  multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1072 #uuid "b6bf65ad-5479-5fb6-8133-3324438581f6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552496 1074 "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ to $A.$ \n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1068 17592186552311 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1069 "q-37656" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x+1?$\n\nI. $x=-6$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1072 #uuid "b7f2915e-8d70-5b64-9329-25d718df5c40" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552497 1074 "- Substituting $-6$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{3}-6(-6)^{2}+9(-6)+1 & = \\\\ -216-216-54+1 & = \\\\ -485\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-6$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-6$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $0$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}+9(0)+1 & = \\\\ 0-0+0+1 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $0$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $0$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}+9(2)+1 & = \\\\ 8-24+18+1 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, there are no roots among the given options, so the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1068 17592186552381 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1069 "q-82378" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1071 "![](images/7d/7d94f4a4ab36e4370754f948d3fa7176c300b057b938faa16ca5843f1d8b42f4.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $5,$ what is the value of $f(132)?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1072 #uuid "b9272833-b47f-50b5-a702-18a31d3e3932" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552498 1074 "Since the period is $5,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $5$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $5$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(132) & =f(132-5) \\\\  & =f(132-2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(132-26\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1068 17592186552371 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1069 "q-144031" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}, & -1<x<3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={(1-X)}^{2},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1072 #uuid "be91f77e-a6ef-5326-b655-78aac371668c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552499 1074 "First, notice that the transformation $Y={(1-X)}^{2}$ is two-to-one over the entire interval $-1<x<3.$ \n\n![](images/03/03718360bf910c74c22b36f34ad2f63bfad6d9387c0cdc62d3fb366a52f356c9.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-1<x<3,$ we have that $Y$ is nonzero on $0\\le y<4.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0\\le y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({(1-X)}^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-{y}^{1/2}\\le 1-X\\le {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =P(-{y}^{1/2}-1\\le -X\\le {y}^{1/2}-1) \\\\  & =P(1-{y}^{1/2}\\le X\\le 1+{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0\\le y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(1+{y}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(1-{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}({f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}({f}_{X}(1+{y}^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(1-{y}^{1/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{1+{y}^{1/2}}{4}+\\frac{1-{y}^{1/2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{y}}, & 0\\le y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1068 17592186552260 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1069 "q-139932" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1071 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1072 #uuid "bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552500 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1068 17592186552293 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1069 "q-222060" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+22x-21$ for $x\\ge 11.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1072 #uuid "c0af0588-6d95-587e-b2d0-7c014fb9577c" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552501 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{2}+22x-21 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-22x)-21 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2}-{11}^{2})-21 \\\\  & =-((x-11)^{2}-121)-21 \\\\  & =-(x-11)^{2}+121-21 \\\\  & =-(x-11)^{2}+100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=-(x-11)^{2}+100\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=-(y-11)^{2}+100\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-(y-11)^{2}+100 \\\\ x-100 & =-(y-11)^{2} \\\\ 100-x & =(y-11)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{100-x} & =y-11 \\\\ \\sqrt{100-x}+11 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 11$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 11.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{100-x}+11\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1068 17592186552305 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1068 17592186552321 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1068 17592186552327 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1069 "q-276050" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1072 #uuid "c1ab528e-b16d-505b-916d-2fee71d7c43b" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552502 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 \\\\ 1+0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1068 17592186552368 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1069 "q-146831" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ students from a class, where $60%$ of the students in the school have an IQ greater than $105.$\n\nII. A sample of $60$ paid TV customers, where $80%$ of the customers watch TV more than $8$ hours per week.\n\nIII. A sample of $40$ players in a team, $90%$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1072 #uuid "c3d4715f-79a5-5718-adbc-5959190456a6" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552503 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.6) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60(0.8) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.8) \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1068 17592186552360 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1069 "q-127063" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xi+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1072 #uuid "c696854b-d1cc-50f5-8398-3fba96da4739" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552504 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1068 17592186552287 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1068 17592186552289 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1069 "q-252169" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=10\\cdot (-5)^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ What is the recursive formula for this sequence? Express your answer in terms of $f(n).$\n\n$f(n+1)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,f(1)=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,n\\ge 1$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1072 #uuid "c9893d5c-f0eb-5a20-a7b0-6d7123b5cbef" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552505 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $f(1).$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10\\cdot (-5)^{1}=10\\cdot (-5)=-50, \\\\ f(2) & =10\\cdot (-5)^{2}=10\\cdot 25=250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{250}{(-50)}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-5f(n),\\,f(1)=-50,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1068 17592186552329 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1069 "q-143052" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-1$ for $x\\le 1.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1072 #uuid "caaab3b2-6a76-5915-aacc-0fc605ea0da3" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552506 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=(x-1)^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=(y-1)^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y-1)^{2}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =(y-1)^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+1} & =y-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{x+1} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=1-\\sqrt{x+1}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1068 17592186552395 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1069 "q-51569" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1071 "![](images/be/be6a289223fdd6c6896278b5e179bf9f4a98d7f9e5988d1b5ae98a1217dd48b0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $3,$ what is the value of $f(30)?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1072 #uuid "cc78fc5e-250d-5649-8a8b-22e66a1b36a4" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552507 1074 "Since the period is $3,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $3$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $3$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(30) & =f(30-3) \\\\  & =f(30-2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(30-10\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =f(0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1068 17592186552393 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1069 "q-109868" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=\\frac{6}{(-2)^{n+1}}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1072 #uuid "cfd63bcb-672d-5a91-b2cf-d24e8a457ef0" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552508 1074 "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $f(1).$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{1+1}}=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{2+1}}=\\frac{6}{(-8)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-\\frac{6}{12}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1068 17592186552356 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1069 "q-113233" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1071 "![](images/82/82adb0c829183451723d0fb4d148f51d5e68b1618faafec9ad5ffb2f4d48682f.png)\n\nWhich of the curves shown above is a graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1072 #uuid "d366ac0b-5786-5e43-9ba3-867d55d183c7" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552509 1074 "The graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd98b8f9b6c7a9260f43772f9c654901bd02c21efa1eb06f2a5fdad8b263729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1068 17592186552306 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1069 "q-124880" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1072 #uuid "d3a52378-b733-50d1-beb6-ebbc02cf54a1" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552510 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1068 17592186552284 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1069 "q-337820" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1072 #uuid "d97c91f1-911f-5cfb-8ef6-5119b61716cb" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552511 1074 "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{2}-4)}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(1-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-4}{1-(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1068 17592186552388 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1069 "q-40077" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1072 #uuid "ddb92873-9866-58c6-b7b0-d5953b026f54" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552512 1074 "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $9$ | $-15$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1}{x-1}=9{x}^{2}-6x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1=(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)=(x-1)(3x-1)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1068 17592186552349 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1069 "q-95061" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=52.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1072 #uuid "e1eb45be-51da-5fe4-b582-bc025ec9533a" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552513 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=52\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=13\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-13.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-13I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]-13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-13)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{aligned}]+13[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1068 17592186552378 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1069 "q-44989" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of an geometric sequence is ${a}_{n}=0.1\\cdot {2}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1072 #uuid "e36afb6e-8973-5d11-97ae-d34a93970838" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552514 1074 "The recursive formula for an geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term ${a}_{1}.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =0.1\\cdot {2}^{1}=0.2, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =0.1\\cdot {2}^{2}=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric  sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1} & =2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=0.2,\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1068 17592186552273 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1069 "q-331046" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1072 #uuid "e53e0594-6789-5dbd-80fa-9b6693e796fe" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552515 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =sin\\,(7\\pi (t+6)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t+42\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡42\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡42\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+6)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-6)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L\\{g(t+6)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1068 17592186552264 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1069 "q-51568" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1071 "![](images/e8/e8f6d5ea1a958c8a6f1a4c06a608d7164c8185c4d306d9783826b88d6503cb7a.png)\n\nPart of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $4$ is shown above. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\le x\\le 4?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1072 #uuid "e6e5881f-1e46-58b4-8c76-656916184499" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552516 1074 "The graph above shows the function in the interval $0\\le x\\le 4.$\n\nSince this interval has length $4,$ and the period of the function is also $4,$ this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $4.$\n\n![](images/eb/eb7f10399368100bdaf83f2701b37c3afa9747ddd907787db21a841752809fb0.png)" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1068 17592186552380 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1069 "q-97869" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1071 "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n\nII. $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. $B$ is non-invertible" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1072 #uuid "e7077a60-6a06-585e-8213-5137d377235a" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552517 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 8+8-2 & =0 \\\\ 14 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is non-invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1068 17592186552373 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1069 "q-41414" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1071 "![](images/b0/b08b9f51e04c33c56026abe338c9e1353ddff93d502596241b31e994d66a994c.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1072 #uuid "e70cf66f-38cb-5302-b354-78492def67e3" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552518 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $8$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $8$ (or a multiple of $8$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $8.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1068 17592186552323 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1069 "q-131462" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1072 #uuid "ed765a29-aa16-50e2-b510-c72034032862" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552519 1074 "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1068 17592186552346 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1069 "q-74360" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1071 "![](images/5e/5e2c5dd5ca5c904ec49a33af3adf9940b400d9f4034ef91b9c7890797da8ba49.png)\n\nWhich of the curves shown above is a graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1072 #uuid "ede3a9d5-128f-518f-842a-1a283c9a8dd9" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552520 1074 "The graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e3f5ed500c81d09e2775f09fd10a221d7ec0fca7e39249b7add7a680328b68c1.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1068 17592186552271 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1068 17592186552339 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1069 "q-131468" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1070 17592186045485 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1071 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1072 #uuid "f30773ae-09b1-517b-85fe-c0701b657a78" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552521 1074 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1068 17592186552390 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1069 "q-110130" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x>1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1072 #uuid "f3a2a8f5-8f7b-5578-9df3-f4e28dce2d10" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552522 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that the possible values of $X$ are $x\\in (1,\\infty ),$ and $Y=\\frac{1}{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$ when $X>1.$\n Moreover, for $x>1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/16/16f3dc78752c8cfcb6b6968e3d57bb3639a4205e2a32dfd569c8c2ca3c29a589.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1068 17592186552379 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1069 "q-100081" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1071 "![](images/ea/ea804b5eb5c4af6aace19a66008dfbf9b130f1e1e38c2d8d9d811e9cad4c413c.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1072 #uuid "f41c0540-27ef-5b47-a626-2808486f0487" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552523 1074 "We find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist, therefore\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)}{f(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{2}-1)}{{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1068 17592186552259 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1069 "q-144914" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2-x}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the probability density function of $Y=(X-4)^{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}g(y), & 0<y<4 \\\\ h(y), & y\\ge 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the function $g(y).$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1072 #uuid "f55a1e43-e983-59db-880e-46aa1036ba76" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552524 1074 "First, let's visualize the transformation $Y=(X-4)^{2}$ over the interval $x\\ge 2.$\n\n![](images/77/77008820197d69dc81d674a48e207b7527d2f3d4cc1d0ca3ed8ad4d8553665b8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The transformation is two-to-one over the interval $2<x<6.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $0<y<4.$\n\n- The transformation is one-to-one over the interval $x\\ge 6.$ On this interval, $Y$ is nonzero on $y\\ge 4.$\n\nIn this question, we're asked to consider only the interval $0<y<4.$\n\nUsing the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P((X-4)^{2}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\sqrt{y}\\le X-4\\le \\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =P(4-\\sqrt{y}\\le X\\le 4+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})-{F}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(4+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(4-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({f}_{X}(4+\\sqrt{y})+{f}_{X}(4-\\sqrt{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}({e}^{2-4-\\sqrt{y}}+{e}^{2-4+\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $g(y)=\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}-2}+{e}^{\\sqrt{y}-2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}.$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1068 17592186552317 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1069 "q-75113" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1070 17592186045486 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations, in the given order, are to be applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1072 #uuid "f5b55192-8108-5b5a-9d5a-dfeb97a067b5" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552525 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the three given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1068 17592186552258 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1069 "q-342628" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1070 17592186045487 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1071 "Find $L\\{u(t-5){e}^{-4(t-5)}\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1072 #uuid "fe74ebec-be9d-5552-b1e8-03aece81d137" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552526 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-5){e}^{-4(t-5)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=5.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-5)={e}^{-4(t-5)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-5)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-(-4)}=\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-5s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-5){e}^{-4(t-5)}\\} & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552527 1062 #uuid "00fbf4c2-2801-5993-ad8e-f84d0f4127a2" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552527 1063 17592186552448 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552528 1058 17592186552553 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552528 1062 #uuid "010efac7-1b1a-535f-9cb9-4b100c90519f" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552528 1063 17592186552436 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552529 1058 17592186552572 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552529 1062 #uuid "02ba616f-857b-5b1b-a652-302fd8658f7d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552529 1063 17592186345055 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552530 1058 17592186552532 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552530 1062 #uuid "05075f15-5b66-59aa-85c7-2761de8fc384" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552530 1063 17592186311739 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552531 1058 17592186552599 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552531 1062 #uuid "065eaab1-7627-57b9-9ca3-6d0fd8d28eef" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552531 1063 17592186552420 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552532 1062 #uuid "092c6a00-72eb-562b-a6ec-edba31e0a817" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552532 1063 17592186396972 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552533 1058 17592186552605 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552533 1062 #uuid "0a4580cf-0850-57eb-9f78-624b36e7297d" 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552533 1063 17592186552465 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552534 1058 17592186552551 13194139535981 17592186045417 true] [17592186552534 1062 #uuid "0bddf274-21d6-5005-a1f9-cd9d602c1a33" 13194139535